From a72310007791722683ba0569bed5a406ad543878 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Avery Felts <averyfelts@aol.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 07:25:57 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 44/44] Windows: run RDTSCP and RDPID under Wine without a
 processor number register

Programs such as Blender execute RDTSCP without checking CPUID, so leaving it
unadvertised did not stop the #UD. Without a register source, ProcessorID now
calls GetCurrentProcessorNumber through a JIT runtime call (as CPUID already
does for its APIC ID), so the value matches what Wine reports, including its
affinity remap. CPUID still advertises RDTSCP and RDPID only with a register
source, since the call is slower (about 45 ns against 9 ns on an M6).

- A failed CPUIndexInTPIDR check is logged as an error, since the user asked
  for it.
- SupportsCPUIndex() is CPUIndexSource != None.

Tested on an Apple M6 (12 CPUs), x86_64, 8 threads, 1.6 million samples:
before, RDTSCP raised #UD; option off, no fault and 1 mismatch (a migration
away and back); option on, the probe passes and 0 mismatches.
